# Rule: SwiftData Model Definition

Sources

https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/model()
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/attribute(_:originalname:hashmodifier:)
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/index(_:)-74ia2
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/index(_:)-7d4z0
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/transient()
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/adopting-inheritance-in-swiftdata
https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftdata/preserving-your-apps-model-data-across-launches

Scope

Applies to:

- `@Model` type declarations
- `@Attribute`, `@Transient`, and index macros
- schema-level uniqueness and rename metadata
- inheritance decisions within persistent model graphs

Intent

Define persistence semantics explicitly in code so schema behavior remains
predictable across migrations, sync, and high-volume query workloads.

Core Principle

Define persistence semantics directly in model code with `@Model` and attribute macros.

Implementation Rules

- Annotate persistable classes with `@Model`.
- Use `@Attribute` only when persistence behavior needs explicit customization
  (for example unique constraints, rename metadata, preserved deletion value behavior).
- Use `@Transient` for stored properties that must not persist.
- Provide default values for non-optional transient stored properties.
- Use `Index` macros for high-value query paths in large datasets.
- Use inheritance only where there is a true model hierarchy and query strategy supports it.
- Avoid deep inheritance for classification that an enum/value strategy can represent.

Canonical Patterns

```swift
@Model
final class Invoice {
    @Attribute(.unique) var id: UUID
    var createdAt: Date

    @Transient var isSelected: Bool = false
}
```

```swift
@Model
final class Quake {
    @Attribute(.unique) var code: String
    var magnitude: Double
}
```

Anti-Patterns

- Treating every property as persistable by default when some are UI-only/transient.
- Adding inheritance where only one boolean/enum distinction exists.
- Introducing unique constraints without migration strategy for existing data.
- Using persistence schema to encode presentation-only state.

Checklist

- Are all persistable classes explicitly `@Model`?
- Are `@Attribute` options justified and migration-safe?
- Are transient properties explicitly marked where needed?
- Are indexes added only for real query needs?
- Is inheritance justified by both domain model and query pattern?

Architectural Implications

- Model declarations are the persistence source of truth and should mirror durable domain semantics, not UI mechanics.
- Attribute/index choices directly shape migration risk and runtime query characteristics.
- Inheritance strategy should be validated against expected query depth and specialization boundaries.

Advanced Notes

- Unique constraints improve local dedup/upsert flows but require migration awareness in existing datasets.
- Transient properties are ideal for ephemeral runtime state that should never drive persistence behavior.
- Index macros should be introduced based on measured query pressure, not proactively everywhere.

Integration With Architecture

- Keep model-definition policy aligned with `Packages/Data/Sources/Data/Persistence/`.
- Avoid embedding view-only flags in persisted entities used by services, actors, or feature roots.
- Coordinate model shape changes with migration files and release rollout strategy.

Enforcement Summary

Always prefer:

- explicit `@Model` declarations for persistable classes
- intentional attribute customization only when needed
- transient markers for non-durable runtime state
- measured index and inheritance decisions

Never allow:

- persistence of presentation-only fields
- schema customization without migration impact review
- inheritance used as classification shortcut without query rationale
- indiscriminate index proliferation
